:answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827/ebfd2493222a3f1fab0cde682da6388804d04c73687b8db82fd88f84cea358de" :answer-field/choices ["q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827/86bbdd527855107550173bb67090db8c6fb79076ff5ce534b85edc33b7b8b5e6" "q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827/ebfd2493222a3f1fab0cde682da6388804d04c73687b8db82fd88f84cea358de" "q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827/80c7962f3a84c8b8e03ae185c1e187dc2e016114800cd2fa86cee299b4d42129" "q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827/fe357c2e6b0879d53c92ee16468899cd291264d3b76d0114f93869f413bcc729" "q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827/d4a937699976a3f1c3cdee342727bcfccbcfc4d5d7fa210144b9866f9ed64d62"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39161"] :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39161/selection/ma-v1/dcd08ea719c740d5f2dca8bb4aee68275f36e767c0a8d752cb00ccc8c7347827"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" :answer/id #uuid "095ac666-2859-585c-92d5-7ff621a2962c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "389bdac7-003e-50b0-b3b2-957bb05dff1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166/selection/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a" :answer/id #uuid "955f9959-4c4f-55d3-bb2f-89d90311e7ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166/selection/1c9cdbfca2809a4bd314efabb96bc8b024a889eaf1c36f67a9f539c735fdc4f4" :answer/id #uuid "922cb98c-f013-533b-a6d2-86acfd8be69f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "3d33cf77-8304-5e85-b136-33eede1e7352" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea42d253-d02a-526e-81d5-5ef1934d7630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39166/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-39166/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" "q-39166/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" "q-39166/selection/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a" "q-39166/selection/1c9cdbfca2809a4bd314efabb96bc8b024a889eaf1c36f67a9f539c735fdc4f4" "q-39166/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39166" :question/id #uuid "cc4a04e7-d61f-575a-ad59-bf6415c59c4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39166" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=4\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4-6\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=4-6\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4-6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6\\text{i} & -3 \\\\ 15 & -3+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 15{x}_{1}+(-3+6\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-39166/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39166"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342/selection/b2d1033481a75a0dc96be2dc60e07f088a51717847861670c7f76c561a0935a3" :answer/id #uuid "4566c5d2-45fc-52c3-a17a-cc61888d2440" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342/selection/0e5452accec41e8d1fd7ef30f755e1450f42ff7c766ddbbbc25c02ca83c6a217" :answer/id #uuid "38f4ace1-d05e-5081-8c4c-133378e8474e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342/selection/0497201a3f4b1abb719d965b0bc5c162796de80a81a144507b0cf24db52996bc" :answer/id #uuid "0ee56d75-5298-5909-b3f9-11a9ea0825b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342/selection/bd54fd94317101fb517b7cf77a0353349ac2a01b8889d2ab48efe98b95fe4a7f" :answer/id #uuid "96c3292b-4ff8-5700-a1ff-bf6149740b94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342/selection/a9f0905453b9f295a80b9d233469e9ce5990ad218d49906fe043966c8bf72362" :answer/id #uuid "349c06d1-4681-57c7-96ee-7c5f3fd4b116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56e5d541-2198-505d-b488-4599ff53d288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99342/selection/0e5452accec41e8d1fd7ef30f755e1450f42ff7c766ddbbbc25c02ca83c6a217" :answer-field/choices ["q-99342/selection/b2d1033481a75a0dc96be2dc60e07f088a51717847861670c7f76c561a0935a3" "q-99342/selection/0e5452accec41e8d1fd7ef30f755e1450f42ff7c766ddbbbc25c02ca83c6a217" "q-99342/selection/0497201a3f4b1abb719d965b0bc5c162796de80a81a144507b0cf24db52996bc" "q-99342/selection/bd54fd94317101fb517b7cf77a0353349ac2a01b8889d2ab48efe98b95fe4a7f" "q-99342/selection/a9f0905453b9f295a80b9d233469e9ce5990ad218d49906fe043966c8bf72362"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99342" :question/id #uuid "5da7514b-8a73-5a50-bb87-67043a1bc3f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99342" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99342/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99342"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498/selection/7495927c269f5774ef34a38108ad0f7feaafe5087423f0add15e2e2f6ebf3408" :answer/id #uuid "a8267c92-6ff8-541c-b37c-708844918388" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498/selection/bd4fe896fdf3e0da3a6eead5def11fddb15b14487bc961ff730d18ddb6b49479" :answer/id #uuid "275c2438-59f4-5507-8f62-29a6b159d1b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498/selection/1f44cfaad37b7ec8904d56d2c1b60237a82b4c40b2dfd41aa01d638471dbbe5a" :answer/id #uuid "3e305e84-e33c-59c1-bd3f-086453fc6408" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498/selection/e2fd3f0db65d7aad9201f4d126f9ad83632de3f38e040b4ca21c7fc6efa34fb6" :answer/id #uuid "c76e0e71-d112-50c4-97a1-8bec0a6ab774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498/selection/b349a0f1ae6a7bb969b22253352caf2bbe5e41a1c67c4184a8096cd17f60199c" :answer/id #uuid "46ab690e-260e-530c-bbb2-16bacaa8c0a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b4e78-d785-58a4-ae44-4f6bd088fed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140498/selection/bd4fe896fdf3e0da3a6eead5def11fddb15b14487bc961ff730d18ddb6b49479" :answer-field/choices ["q-140498/selection/7495927c269f5774ef34a38108ad0f7feaafe5087423f0add15e2e2f6ebf3408" "q-140498/selection/bd4fe896fdf3e0da3a6eead5def11fddb15b14487bc961ff730d18ddb6b49479" "q-140498/selection/1f44cfaad37b7ec8904d56d2c1b60237a82b4c40b2dfd41aa01d638471dbbe5a" "q-140498/selection/e2fd3f0db65d7aad9201f4d126f9ad83632de3f38e040b4ca21c7fc6efa34fb6" "q-140498/selection/b349a0f1ae6a7bb969b22253352caf2bbe5e41a1c67c4184a8096cd17f60199c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140498" :question/id #uuid "1a9407fa-0156-51d0-868b-a6bb68a8909c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140498" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140498/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140498"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "da98d65a-6485-5302-a2fa-bb9cfbed7d73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "a7c55d19-794a-5188-8bae-067da7ce929d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "049eb50a-4d0c-5bd3-b9a4-b1c9d6b6f4b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d20a6bb5-547c-5c0d-81f1-591bf52af8ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8d0325e8-8b6c-586d-b83b-d336b14159f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e5f5d4b-ae66-5809-9811-870151c8154f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140501/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-140501/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-140501/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-140501/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-140501/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-140501/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140501" :question/id #uuid "b71065be-44ce-5f43-8458-63be59f4de8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140501" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140501/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140501"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
